Problem: NUCLEO-H743ZI EEPROM_Emulation Bug
Context: repo=STM32CubeH7, mcu_series=H7, board=NUCLEO-H743ZI, component=None, layer=Other, issue_kind=bug_report, severity=high, evidence_strength=high, rescue_applied=False, state=closed, issue_number=309
Root cause: Hello @Gorden1970, I tried to run the EEPROM_Emulation example on NUCLEO-H743ZI board using **IAR version 9.
Fix: \Projects\NUCLEO-H743ZI\Applications\EEPROM\EEPROM_Emulation\Src\eeprom.
Constraints: Validate against exact MCU/board configuration and CubeMX/CubeH7 version before applying.
Keywords: H7, NUCLEO-H743ZI, bug_report, high, bug, internal bug tracker, projects, EEPROM, EEPROM_Emulation, EE_VerifyPageFullWriteVariable, SCB_EnableICache

Technical details:
\Projects\NUCLEO-H743ZI\Applications\EEPROM\EEPROM_Emulation\Src\eeprom.c, EE_VerifyPageFullWriteVariable function SCB_EnableICache() was issued at an inappropriate location [IMAGE DESCRIPTION] Screenshot of a C source file in an embedded firmware project, showing part of an EEPROM emulation routine for STM32 flash programming. The code loops through active page addresses, checks whether the current flash word at Address is erased (0xFFFFFFFF), programs variable data at Address, then programs a virtual address at Address + 32. If flash programming fails, it returns the HAL error status; otherwise it advances Address by 64 bytes in the else branch. The visible snippet also disables instruction cache before the loop and re-enables it after the loop. This appears relevant to a flash write/EEPROM emulation bug on NUCLEO-H743ZI involving address handling and programming flow. [IMAGE TEXT] 654 SCB_DisableICache();
655
656 /* Check each active page address starting from beginning */
657 while (Address < PageEndAddress)
658 {
659 /* Verify if Address and Address+2 contents are 0xFFFFFFFF */
660 if ((*(__IO uint32_t*)Address) == 0xFFFFFFFF)
661 {
662 /* Set variable data */
663 FlashStatus = HAL_FLASH_Program(FLASH_TYPEPROGRAM_FLASHWORD, Address, ((uint32_t)data32));
664
665 /* If program operation was failed, a Flash error code is returned */
666 if (FlashStatus != HAL_OK)
667 {
668 return FlashStatus;
669 }
670 /* Set variable virtual address */
671 FlashStatus = HAL_FLASH_Program(FLASH_TYPEPROGRAM_FLASHWORD, Address + 32, ((uint32_t)VirtAddress1));
672
673 /* Return program operation status */
674 return FlashStatus; /* Gorden1970: If the Flash program executes successfully, SCB_EnableICache() will not be called */
675 }
676 else
677 {
678 /* Next address location */
679 Address = Address + 64;
680 }
681 }
682 SCB_EnableICache();

Key comments:
Hello @Gorden1970, I tried to run the EEPROM_Emulation example on NUCLEO-H743ZI board using **IAR version 9.40.1** and achieved the expected behavior as described in the [readme.md](https://github.com/STMicroelectronics/STM32CubeH7/blob/master/Projects/NUCLEO-H743ZI/Applications/EEPROM/EEPROM_Emulation/readme.txt) file. Could you please give me more details about the issue you're experiencing and how I might be able to reproduce it? In my test steps, as long as I call EE_VerifyPageFullWriteVariable, I will find that the overall performance is reduced. Finally, I found that the SCB_EnableICache I originally set would be changed to SCB_DisableICache Hello @Gorden1970, After discussing with our experts, they explained that disabling the I-Cache before performing flash program/erase operations is essential to ensure data integrity and proper execution. This is why the SCB_EnableICache() and SCB_DisableICache() functions were added to the EE_VerifyPageFullWriteVariable() API. However, your concern is valid—frequent enabling and disabling of the instruction cache (I-Cache) can lead to performance degradation, as it is indeed a resource-intensive operation. I hope this addresses you

Problem: RBU error because of bug in stm32h7xx_hal_eth.c
Context: repo=STM32CubeH7, mcu_series=H7, board=STM32H7XX, component=None, layer=HAL, issue_kind=bug_report, severity=medium, evidence_strength=high, rescue_applied=False, state=closed, issue_number=307
Root cause: Hello @nrg83, Thank you for your report.
Fix: The problem is this line of code in stm32h7xx_hal_eth.
Constraints: Validate against exact MCU/board configuration and CubeMX/CubeH7 version before applying.
Keywords: H7, STM32H7XX, HAL, bug_report, medium, bug, spotted before customer, eth, ETH_RX_DESC_CNT, RBU, ETH_DMACRXDTPR, ETH_DMACCARXDR

Technical details:
The problem is this line of code in stm32h7xx_hal_eth.c i think. tailidx = (descidx + 1U) % ETH_RX_DESC_CNT; The descriptor list is organized as a ring buffer, e.g. desc[0], desc[1], desc[2], desc[3], desc[4], desc[5] The tail pointer must set "behind" the current descriptor, not in front of it. E.g. current descriptor index = 2, tail pointer must be set to 1, and not 3! RBU occurs when ETH_DMACRXDTPR = ETH_DMACCARXDR You can try this fix maybe. [IMAGE DESCRIPTION] Screenshot of a C source code excerpt from stm32h7xx_hal_eth.c showing a suspected fix for Ethernet RX descriptor tail pointer handling. The code is inside a conditional checking if `heth->RxDescList.RxBuildDescCnt != desccount`, then sets the tail pointer index. A previous formula is commented out as a TODO (`tailidx = (descidx + 1U) % ETH_RX_DESC_CNT;`), and a “Possible fix?” branches on `if (descidx == 0)` to set `tailidx = ETH_RX_DESC_CNT - 1;` otherwise `tailidx = (descidx - 1U) % ETH_RX_DESC_CNT;`. Red wavy underlines indicate spell-check/editor highlighting on some identifiers and comments. [IMAGE TEXT] 1218 if (heth->RxDescList.RxBuildDescCnt != desccount)
1219 {
1220 /* Set the tail pointer index */
1221 //TODO tailidx = (descidx + 1U) % ETH_RX_DESC_CNT;
1222
1223 // Possible fix?
1224 if (descidx == 0)
1225 {
1226 tailidx = ETH_RX_DESC_CNT - 1;
1227 }
1228 else
1229 {
1230 tailidx = (descidx - 1U) % ETH_RX_DESC_CNT;
1231 }
1232 } You can also check this comment. Are you sure you save the index here? [IMAGE DESCRIPTION] Cropped code editor view from a C source file showing lines 1094–1095. The visible snippet contains a comment indicating the last descriptor index is being saved, followed by an assignment to a network receive descriptor list field: the previous Rx descriptor pointer/index is set to the current descriptor value labeled DESC3. This appears related to STM32 Ethernet HAL receive buffer/descriptor handling. [IMAGE TEXT] 1094 /* Save Last descriptor index */
1095 heth->RxDescList.pRxLastRxDesc = dmardesc->DESC3;

Key comments:
Hello @nrg83, Thank you for your report. This bug has been fixed in commit [ceda3ce](https://github.com/STMicroelectronics/stm32h7xx_hal_driver/commit/ceda3ceeca2ee77a76d2de2ee6b560ad87c90a48) by correcting the calculation of the tail pointer so that it points to the last updated descriptor. Hello @nrg83, Please allow me to close this issue.

